WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.160
How to deal with people who dominate the&nbsp;
conversation? I have dealt with this so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.800
much in my work. I've learned some really clever&nbsp;
techniques, I've made some stumbles, blunders and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:17.680
failures and in this video, I'm going to show you&nbsp;
3 of my favorite methods for... in the gentlest,

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.120
most loving possible way, quieting people down&nbsp;
who tend to dominate conversation to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.480
space for all voices to be heard. This is really&nbsp;
good if you have any interest in creating more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.400
psychologically safe and inclusive environments&nbsp;
in your own educational leadership group context.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.669
I'm Chad Littlefield, let's get into it.

00:00:44.645 --> 00:00:49.116
A big part of my past work
experience had led me to

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.840
creating We and Me, these video tutorials, being&nbsp;
invited to speak and facilitate workshops all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.840
around the country, was working with this group&nbsp;
called World In Conversation where my job was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.840
sit in over 500 dialogues and lead&nbsp;
conversations with groups of 6 to 10 people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.800
on topics that were fairly contentious, race&nbsp;
relations, long-term conflict, gender, politics etc.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.560
Very frequently, somebody would really dominate&nbsp;
those conversations and it might be, you know, they

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.720
might not have bad intentions, they might just&nbsp;
like really love to talk and they love to hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.400
the sound of their own voice or they're really&nbsp;
passionate about a particular topic and they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:29.040
just constantly sharing, which is, it's not always&nbsp;
bad, but it is bad when it suppresses other voices.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.640
To be a little bit vulnerable in this moment,&nbsp;
like, I love to share,  love to talk and so I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.960
recognizing that even as I'm recording this video&nbsp;
sometimes, I am the monopolizer. And so, I've learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:50.720
through some deep empathy how to really re-guide&nbsp;
and direct traffic toward all voices in a room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.640
not just the monopolizer, not just the person who&nbsp;
dominates a conversation. I'm gonna call my first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:01.600
strategy Flip the Roles. This is so easy to do&nbsp;
and it is so powerful. All I do in a group...

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.120
so let's say we're having a conversation, it could&nbsp;
be virtual could be in-person, and noticing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.040
somebody's really dominating the conversation&nbsp;
and I'm about to ask for people to participate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:17.440
or share, ask people's perspective, all I'm gonna do&nbsp;
is say, "Hey, this time when I ask you the question

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.360
'what struck you about those conversations?' or 'what&nbsp;
struck you about that last meeting?' or 'what do you

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.720
think about this?'" When I'm about to do that, right&nbsp;
before I ask that question, I say, "If you typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.320
are the first person to speak or one of the first&nbsp;
3 people to speak, hold off and be like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.320
8th person to speak and it's possible you might&nbsp;
not get a chance to speak this time, but I'd love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.480
to hear some of the voices that don't always&nbsp;
get heard." And so, did you see what I did there?

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.840
People who talk a lot, know they talk a lot,&nbsp;
and so I just said flip the roles. I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.200
say, "We don't want to hear from you," I said, "We'd&nbsp;
love to hear from you, but not in a way of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.120
hey if you haven't spoken yet, like hey, like Sandra&nbsp;
and Mark and Jamal, I haven't heard from either of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.480
the 3 of you, can you like figure this out and&nbsp;
share something," right? No, I'm not putting them on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.720
the spot. I'm inviting loud people to be quieter&nbsp;
by saying that I'd love to hear from other people.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.680
That simple flip the role is so powerful.&nbsp;
Now, related to that is a technique that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:22.720
would call Creating Space, and like space, like&nbsp;
outer space, it's silent. And sometimes if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:29.280
leave just a little bit of silence, it creates&nbsp;
enough of a moment for someone who tends to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.800
a little bit more hesitant to share to actually&nbsp;
share. And so, one of the ways that I create space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.360
is by saying, you know, and I really think that the&nbsp;
best way to avoid awkward silence is to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.880
productive silence, and so one of the ways that&nbsp;
I would create space with a group is just to say,

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.920
"Hey, I'm going to ask you a question.&nbsp;
I'd love to get all of your responses.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.120
If you tend to be the first person to speak, hold&nbsp;
off and maybe be the 3rd, 4th or 5th person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.160
to speak because I'd love to hear from everybody&nbsp;
in the group. I'm going to ask you this question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.560
and then I would love to just pause in&nbsp;
total silence for 5 seconds to wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.800
for everybody to think of their own response&nbsp;
before we popcorn out a handful of responses."

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.240
And then I share and in that 5 seconds,&nbsp;
you've created enough space for the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.480
that are a little bit more hesitant to say, "Okay, I&nbsp;
think this is what I want to say," and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.840
jump in, right? And even if no one shares in that&nbsp;
5 seconds, let that time sit. It is such a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.960
exercise for monopolizers to just sit there and&nbsp;
bear through silence and also if it gets really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:34.080
bad, I might reflect back to the monopolizers that&nbsp;
they're like, "Well, there's so much silence, I just,

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.640
I had to jump in and say something." I might reflect&nbsp;
back to them and was like, "It was actually only 4

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.240
seconds of quiet and so let's do this one more&nbsp;
time and let's just take 10 seconds of total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.400
silence and get everybody a proper chance to think."&nbsp;
Not everybody gets ready to speak as quickly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.520
some. The 2nd technique that I'm going to share&nbsp;
with you is inviting you to change the mechanics.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.360
If there's somebody monopolizing conversation or&nbsp;
dominating a conversation, it might be because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:04.160
them, but it also might because&nbsp;of
your structure. If you're meeting in a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.720
group of 12 people, you're breaking Amazon's 2&nbsp;
pizza rule which is never have more people at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.200
meeting than 2 pizzas can feed. And&nbsp;
so, that structure would be lousy. If there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.000
too many cooks in the kitchen, you're just&nbsp;
asking for somebody to dominate and take over

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.920
and asking for everybody else to just sit into&nbsp;
the role of consumer. And so, when I say "change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.600
the mechanics," what I mean by that is split&nbsp;
out into groups of 3 to have discussions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.560
and then come back and report out what&nbsp;
you heard other people in your group say,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.560
not what you said. Because when you have people&nbsp;
report out what other people said, the monopolizers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:44.400
don't go quite as crazy with other people's words&nbsp;
as with their own. Whereas if you are asking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.160
their opinion, they'll give you that for days on&nbsp;
days. 3rd technique here, and we're starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.080
to get down to the wire, we're starting to get down&nbsp;
to the place where like if the first 2 ideas or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.920
techniques didn't work, the 2nd one is a little&nbsp;
bit more of a direct approach, it's very effective,

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.120
there's a a really gentle kind way to do it&nbsp;
and then there's a blunt way that is like kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.800
of my last ditch. Not last ditch effort, it's my&nbsp;
strategic way and this is steering into the curve.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.480
So, if you're not familiar with that metaphor,
if&nbsp;you're in a car and it's spinning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:20.880
out of control on icy roads or something around&nbsp;
a curve, you want to steer into the curve, which

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.840
is very counterintuitive, right? You're actually&nbsp;
going to what feels like toward danger to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.640
yourself realigned. And if that metaphor's not&nbsp;
working for you, pointing out the elephant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:37.680
in the room is this idea. And so, just actually&nbsp;
calling out the dynamic privately first. So,, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.480
a gentle loving way to... if you notice somebody's&nbsp;
like really dominating every conversation,

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:48.560
to pull somebody aside and use technique&nbsp;
number 1 in private one-on-one with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.360
person dominating. And so, actually look at them and&nbsp;
say, "Hey, what I'd love for you to do... I'm loving all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.400
your contributions and I'm recognizing that your&nbsp;
contributions are heavier than other people's and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:04.640
I want to make space for other people's voices.&nbsp;
And so, can you aim to just like bear through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:10.800
a little bit of awkward silence to give space for&nbsp;
other people to share for the rest of our day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.960
or for the rest of our session etc.?" That could be&nbsp;
done if you have some trust and rapport with them,

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.600
that can be done in direct message over Zoom, that&nbsp;
could be done in a side conversation on a break.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.400
You choose how to do that, but that private&nbsp;
check-in, steering to the curve and just pointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:28.800
out the dynamic because guess what? You're not&nbsp;
going to surprise them. It's like, I once worked...

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.600
I did a this whole team development program with&nbsp;
a group of students who were blind and I was like,

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.840
"Oh my gosh like I'm not blind, this is&nbsp;
so not normal." And I realized like they taught me,

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:43.920
"Oh, this is totally normal for them." Like me&nbsp;
pointing out that they're blind or that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:49.360
visually impaired is not news to them. So, pointing&nbsp;
out to somebody who's dominating a conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:53.360
that they're dominating a conversation isn't news&nbsp;
to them, right? You're not going to shock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.200
them, you're not going to like, make them enrage,&nbsp;
they're not going to spin off and go crazy because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.000
you shared that. And the 2nd way to steer into&nbsp;
the curve or point at the elephant in the room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.760
is to do that publicly in front of the group and&nbsp;
I'd be really mindful of this and I'll share an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:09.120
example of how I did this recently. It was a bit&nbsp;
of a risk. So, I was in a workshop and it was about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:17.280
80% women and 4 guys, all of them were&nbsp;
white and there were a handful of people color on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:23.040
the call as well. I was noticing that me and Will&nbsp;
who were facilitating, my co-founder, were talking a

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.840
bunch and then this one other person who was also&nbsp;
a white guy was talking a bunch and what came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.120
me in that moment when he went to speak, and he&nbsp;
was kind of dominating the conversation a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:35.920
bit, not a bad way. It was great contributions,&nbsp;
but his voice was heavier and I said, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.800
wouldn't mind me sharing this, his name's Jason.&nbsp;
I just said, "Hey, Jason. Quick pause. I recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.040
we're playing white guy ping pong right now. I'd&nbsp;
love to create some space to hear other voices in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.200
the group." After that conversation I had multiple&nbsp;
individuals message me and say, "Oh my gosh. I never

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:53.600
heard a white guy call out other white guys for&nbsp;
playing white guy ping pong like that, that was so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:58.480
awesome. I felt so heard and seen and it allowed&nbsp;
and created a space for my voice." And so, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:03.520
an example of how I might publicly acknowledge&nbsp;
that in also a really loving way though. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.680
love creating awesome conversations, you would&nbsp;
love this book and all the tools that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:16.640
available for free digital download in the link&nbsp;
